# TimelessMetrics Architecture

## Operating Modes

TimelessMetrics supports three operating modes with a unified pipeline:

| Mode | Config | Storage | Use Case |
|------|--------|---------|----------|
| **Server** | `data_dir: "/data"` | ETS + disk (.seg files) | Standalone TSDB, HTTP API, scraping |
| **Embedded** | `data_dir: "/data"` | ETS + disk (.seg files) | Library inside an Elixir app |
| **Memory-only** | `mode: :memory` | ETS only, no disk | Routers, constrained devices, testing |

```elixir
# Server / Embedded (same code, different deployment)
{TimelessMetrics, name: :metrics, data_dir: "/data/metrics"}

# Memory-only (no disk, no SQLite)
{TimelessMetrics, name: :metrics, mode: :memory}
```

All three modes share the same write and query paths. The only difference is whether
compressed segments are persisted to disk for crash recovery.

## Supervision Tree

```mermaid
graph TD
    App["TimelessMetrics.Application<br/><i>one_for_one</i>"]
    App --> Sup
    App --> HTTP["TimelessMetrics.HTTP<br/><i>Rocket HTTP server</i>"]

    Sup["TimelessMetrics.Supervisor<br/><i>rest_for_one</i>"]

    Sup --> DB["TimelessMetrics.DB<br/><i>SQLite (server mode only)</i>"]
    Sup --> SR["TimelessMetrics.SeriesRegistry<br/><i>persistent_term + ETS overflow</i>"]
    Sup --> DT["TimelessMetrics.DictTrainer<br/><i>server mode only</i>"]
    Sup --> SB0["SegmentBuilder 0<br/><i>compression + ETS cache</i>"]
    Sup --> BUF0["Buffer 0<br/><i>ETS write shard</i>"]
    Sup --> SBN["SegmentBuilder N<br/><i>compression + ETS cache</i>"]
    Sup --> BUFN["Buffer N<br/><i>ETS write shard</i>"]
    Sup --> IW["IngestWorker × N<br/><i>server mode only</i>"]
    Sup --> Rollup["TimelessMetrics.Rollup"]
    Sup --> Ret["TimelessMetrics.Retention"]
    Sup --> AE["TimelessMetrics.AlertEvaluator<br/><i>server mode only</i>"]
    Sup --> SelfMon["TimelessMetrics.SelfMonitor<br/><i>optional</i>"]
    Sup --> ScrapeSup["DynamicSupervisor<br/><i>server mode only</i>"]

    style DB stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style DT stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style IW stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style AE stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style ScrapeSup stroke-dasharray: 5 5
    style SelfMon stroke-dasharray: 5 5
```

Dashed components are skipped in memory-only mode (no DB, no IngestWorkers, no
AlertEvaluator, no Scraper). SeriesRegistry operates without SQLite — ETS +
persistent_term only, no restart durability.

## Write Path — HTTP Ingest (Server Mode)

HTTP import endpoints accept the raw body, zstd-compress it, and insert it into a
shared ETS queue table. The response returns **204 immediately**.

Background `IngestWorker` processes drain the queue every 10ms, decompress, parse
(PrometheusNif or JSON), resolve series IDs, and write to Buffer ETS.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph "HTTP Accept (immediate 204)"
        REQ["POST /api/v1/import/prometheus"]
        REQ --> ZSTD["ezstd.compress(body, 2)"]
        ZSTD --> Q[":ets.insert(queue, {seq, compressed_body, format})"]
    end

    subgraph "IngestWorker (background, every 10ms)"
        Q -.->|":ets.take (atomic)"| IW["IngestWorker.process_entries"]
        IW --> DECOMP["ezstd.decompress"]
        DECOMP --> PARSE["PrometheusNif.parse (dirty CPU scheduler)"]
        PARSE --> RESOLVE["SeriesRegistry.get_or_create (lock-free)"]
        RESOLVE --> SHARD["rem(abs(series_id), shard_count)"]
        SHARD --> BUF[":ets.insert(shard_table, {{sid, ts, seq}, value})"]
    end

    subgraph "Series Resolution (lock-free)"
        RESOLVE --> PT[":persistent_term lookup"]
        PT -->|hit| SID["Return series_id"]
        PT -->|miss| OVERFLOW[":ets.lookup(overflow)"]
        OVERFLOW -->|hit| SID
        OVERFLOW -->|miss| CAS[":atomics.add_get + :ets.insert_new"]
        CAS --> SID
        CAS -.->|"cast (async)"| SQLite["GenServer queues SQLite INSERT"]
    end
```

**Data is queryable the instant it enters Buffer ETS.** No flush required.

## Write Path — Programmatic API (All Modes)

`TimelessMetrics.write/5` resolves the series ID and inserts directly into the
Buffer shard's ETS table. No GenServer, no message passing. `write_batch/2`
parallelizes across schedulers for batches over 1,000 entries.

This is the only write path for memory-only mode (no HTTP, no IngestWorkers).

## Buffer → SegmentBuilder → ETS Cache (→ Disk)

The Buffer has **no periodic flush timer**. Data lives in Buffer ETS (queryable
immediately) until a 4-hour seal cycle runs or an explicit flush is triggered.

Every **4 hours**, the SegmentBuilder's `check_segments` timer fires:

1. Tells the paired Buffer to flush all points to SegmentBuilder (sync call)
2. SegmentBuilder accumulates points grouped by `{series_id, time_bucket}`
3. Seals completed 4-hour windows: compress with ALP + zstd
4. Stores compressed blobs in the **ETS segment cache** (queryable immediately)
5. **Server mode only**: also persists to .seg files on disk for crash recovery
6. Pending (current window) segments stay in SegmentBuilder memory

On shutdown, `terminate` flushes Buffer and writes ALL remaining segments.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph "Buffer ETS (queryable immediately, no timer)"
        BUF["Ordered ETS: {sid, ts, seq} → value<br/><i>Data lives here until 4-hour seal cycle</i>"]
    end

    subgraph "Seal Cycle (every 4 hours)"
        BUF -->|"flush_sync"| SB["SegmentBuilder accumulates<br/>{sid, bucket} → points"]
        SB --> SPLIT{"Time window<br/>completed?"}
        SPLIT -->|yes| COMPRESS["ALP + zstd compress"]
        COMPRESS --> CACHE["ETS Segment Cache<br/><i>compressed blobs, queryable</i>"]
        CACHE -->|"server mode"| DISK["Write .seg file (durability)"]
        SPLIT -->|no| KEEP["Keep in SegmentBuilder memory"]
    end

    subgraph "Also triggers flush"
        TERM["terminate (shutdown)"]
        EXPLICIT["TimelessMetrics.flush(store)"]
    end
```

### Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|----------|-----------|
| No Buffer flush timer | Real scrapes are 1-5 min intervals; sub-minute timers waste CPU and produce tiny blocks |
| 4-hour seal cycle | Matches segment duration; accumulates 240+ points/series at 1-min scrape for good ALP compression |
| Timer offset from process start | Avoids hour-boundary scrape storms when every Prometheus target fires at :00 |
| Buffer ETS queryable | Hot data available instantly; no flush-to-query gap |
| ETS segment cache | ALL reads go through ETS — never touch disk. Disk is write-only for durability |
| WAL merge: replace by `{sid, start}` | Each write is the complete segment — safe to replace |
| Same pipeline for all modes | Memory-only skips disk write; everything else identical |

## Read / Query Path

Queries read from **two ETS sources** and merge the results:

1. **Buffer ETS** — hot, uncompressed points (O(log n) tree walk on ordered_set)
2. **ETS Segment Cache** — compressed ALP blobs (O(log n) ordered_set lookup, then decompress)

**Reads never touch disk.** Disk files (.seg) are only for crash recovery on startup,
when they are loaded into the ETS segment cache.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q["Query.raw(store, series_id, from, to)"]

    Q --> CACHE["ETS Segment Cache (lock-free)"]
    Q --> ETS["Buffer.read_points (lock-free)"]

    subgraph "Segment Cache Read"
        CACHE --> LOOKUP["ordered_set: {sid, start} → {end, count, blob}"]
        LOOKUP --> DECOMP["ALP/Gorilla decompress"]
    end

    subgraph "Buffer ETS Read"
        ETS --> WALK[":ets.next walk from {sid, from, 0}"]
    end

    DECOMP --> MERGE["lists.merge(buffer_points, cache_points)"]
    WALK --> MERGE
    MERGE --> RESULT["{:ok, [{ts, val}, ...]}"]
```

**No GenServer in the read path.** Both ETS tables are `:public` with `read_concurrency: true`.

For multi-series queries, `find_matching_series` reads from persistent_term + ETS overflow
(no SQLite on the hot path), and fans out in parallel via `Task.async_stream`.

Aggregate queries use a tier-aware router: if rollup data exists (hourly/daily tiers),
it reads from rollup chunks. If the query spans the watermark boundary, it stitches
rollup data with raw data.

## Storage Layout

### In-Memory (both modes)

```
Per-Shard ETS Tables:
  Buffer ETS        — raw points: {sid, ts, seq} → value (ordered_set)
  Segment Cache ETS — compressed: {sid, start} → {end, count, blob} (ordered_set)
```

### On-Disk (server mode only, for crash recovery)

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph "Per-Shard Directory (shard_N/)"
        subgraph "raw/"
            WAL["current.wal<br/><i>pending compressed segments</i>"]
            SEG1["1706000000.seg<br/><i>sealed 4-hour window</i>"]
            SEGN["1706014400.seg<br/><i>sealed 4-hour window</i>"]
        end
        subgraph "tier_hourly/"
            TH["chunks.dat + index.ets"]
        end
        subgraph "tier_daily/"
            TD["chunks.dat + index.ets"]
        end
    end

    subgraph "SQLite metrics.db"
        Series["series<br/>id, metric_name, labels, created_at"]
        Metadata["metric_metadata<br/>metric_name, type, unit, description"]
        AlertRules["alert_rules + alert_state + alert_history"]
        ScrapeTargets["scrape_targets + scrape_health"]
        Annotations["annotations<br/>id, timestamp, title, tags"]
    end
```

On server startup, all .seg and WAL data is loaded into the ETS segment cache.
After startup, disk is write-only — reads never touch it.

### Compression Pipeline

| Series Type | Encoder | Container | Marker Byte |
|-------------|---------|-----------|-------------|
| Numeric | ALP (Adaptive Lossless floating-Point) | zstd level 2 | `0xA1` |
| Text | RLE (run-length encoding) | zstd | `0xFE` |
| Single point | Raw (8B ts + 8B val) | none | `0xA2` |
| Legacy | Gorilla (delta-of-delta + XOR) | zstd | default / `0xFF` |

### Segment File Format (.seg)

```
Header: "TS" (2) | version (1) | entry_count (4) | index_offset (8) = 15 bytes
Body:   concatenated compressed blobs
Index:  40 bytes per entry (sid:i64, start:i64, end:i64, count:u32, offset:u64, len:u32)
```

## Timers and Thresholds

| Component | Timer/Threshold | Default Value | Purpose |
|-----------|----------------|---------------|---------|
| IngestWorker | Drain interval | 10ms | Poll ETS queue for new HTTP bodies |
| IngestWorker | Max batch | 100 entries | Entries per drain cycle |
| SegmentBuilder | Seal cycle | 4 hours | Flush Buffer, compress, seal completed windows |
| SegmentBuilder | Segment duration | 14,400s (4h) | Time window per .seg file |
| SeriesRegistry | Publish interval | 5,000ms | Merge ETS overflow → persistent_term |

**No timers in the Buffer.** Data moves from Buffer to compressed cache only on the
4-hour seal cycle, explicit flush, or shutdown.

## HTTP Ingest and Query

`TimelessMetrics.HTTP` is served by Rocket (custom HTTP server using OTP's `:socket`
module + picohttpparser NIF). Ingest endpoints accept VictoriaMetrics JSON lines and
Prometheus text exposition format. Bodies are queued for background processing —
**the HTTP handler never parses, resolves, or writes data synchronously**.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph Ingest
        VMJ["POST /api/v1/import<br/>VM JSON lines"]
        Prom["POST /api/v1/import/prometheus<br/>Prometheus text"]

        VMJ --> ENQUEUE["zstd compress → ETS queue → 204"]
        Prom --> ENQUEUE
    end

    subgraph Query
        Exp["GET /api/v1/export<br/>raw points"]
        QLatest["GET /api/v1/query<br/>latest value"]
        QRange["GET /api/v1/query_range<br/>aggregated range"]
        PromQL["GET /prometheus/api/v1/query_range<br/>PromQL (Grafana)"]

        Exp --> QEng["Query.raw → Buffer ETS + Segment Cache ETS"]
        QLatest --> QEng
        QRange --> QEng
        PromQL --> PQL["PromQL.parse → PromQL.execute"]
        PQL --> QEng
        QEng --> Resp["JSON response"]
    end

    subgraph Operational
        Health["GET /health<br/>Stats.snapshot + SeriesRegistry.count"]
        Backup["POST /api/v1/backup"]
        Metrics["GET /metrics<br/>Prometheus exposition"]
    end

    Auth["Bearer token check"] -.->|guards| Ingest
    Auth -.->|guards| Query
```

## Scraper Subsystem

The `Scraper` GenServer manages target CRUD and worker lifecycle (server mode only).
On init it loads enabled targets from SQLite and starts a `Scraper.Worker` under
`DynamicSupervisor` for each. Workers schedule scrapes on fixed intervals with initial
jitter to avoid thundering herd. Each scrape tick fetches the target URL via HTTP,
parses the Prometheus exposition text, applies the metric relabel pipeline, and writes
the resulting entries through `TimelessMetrics.write_batch`.

## Alert System

The `AlertEvaluator` GenServer ticks every 60 seconds (server mode only) and calls
`Alert.evaluate/1`. For each enabled rule, it queries recent metric data via
`query_aggregate_multi`, then checks the last bucket value against the rule's condition
(`above`/`below` threshold). A per-series state machine tracks transitions through
`ok → pending → firing → resolved`. State changes to `firing` or `resolved` trigger
async webhook delivery and are logged to `alert_history`.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> ok
    ok --> pending: breaching AND duration > 0
    ok --> firing: breaching AND duration = 0
    ok --> ok: not breaching
    pending --> firing: breaching AND elapsed >= duration
    pending --> pending: breaching AND elapsed < duration
    pending --> ok: not breaching
    firing --> firing: still breaching
    firing --> resolved: not breaching
    resolved --> pending: breaching AND duration > 0
    resolved --> firing: breaching AND duration = 0
    resolved --> ok: not breaching

    note right of firing: webhook dispatched on entry
    note right of resolved: webhook dispatched on entry
```
